<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Lacetani</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 195 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Iberian people of inland Catalonia who resisted Rome during the Second Punic War and Cato's campaign of 195 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Lacetani lived in the interior of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Catalonia</a>, roughly in the modern comarques of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bages">Bages</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anoia">Anoia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solsones">Solsonès</a>, from about the 5th century BCE. They were part of the Iberian culture of the north-east and neighbours of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ilergetes">Ilergetes</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ausetani">Ausetani</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their society of fortified hilltop villages lived from farming and herding. Ancient writers, mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, record them only in wartime, and their name is easily confused with the coastal <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/laietani">Laietani</a>, so many details are uncertain.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>They fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-cornelius-scipio-calvus">Gnaeus Scipio</a> in 218 BCE and again in the north-eastern revolt of 197 to 195 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-elder">Cato</a> stormed their chief town. Afterwards they were absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a> and their name faded.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lacetani.jpg" alt="Lacetani" width="320" height="169"><figcaption>Image: Joan Porredon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moneda_Sikarra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 195 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Iberian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Iberian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lacetanians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-348&amp;focus=lacetani" aria-label="Lacetani on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4611 727 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lacetani lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5031 967h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4931 983h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4931 983h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Spain. <a href="/atlas?year=-348&amp;focus=lacetani">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
